Email Links ⭐⭐
You can make a link that opens the user's email application:
<a href="mailto:someone@example.com">
    Email Me
</a>
The important part is:
mailto:
This is different from:
<a href="https://...">
because you're specifying an email address rather than a webpage.


7. Telephone Links⭐⭐⭐
You can also create a telephone link:
<a href="tel:+911234567890">
    Call Me
</a>
On compatible devices, clicking it can open the phone/dialing interface.


8.Linking to a Specific Section of the Same Page ⭐⭐⭐
Suppose you have:
<h2 id="contact">Contact Us</h2>


You can create a link to it:
<a href="#contact">Go to Contact</a>


Notice:
#contact
The # means you're referring to an element's ID on the current page.
So:
<h2 id="contact">Contact Us</h2>
and:
<a href="#contact">Go to Contact</a>
work together.


9. Linking to a Section on Another Page
You can combine relative URLs and IDs.
Suppose:
about.html
contains:

<h2 id="team">Our Team</h2>

From another page:

<a href="about.html#team">Meet Our Team</a>

The browser:
Opens about.html
Finds id="team"
Jumps to that section.




17. <figure> and <figcaption> ⭐⭐

When an image is a self-contained piece of content that needs a caption, you can use:

<figure>
    <img src="mountain.jpg" alt="Snow-covered mountain">
    <figcaption>Mountain landscape</figcaption>
</figure>

Here:

<figure>       → groups the image/content
<img>          → image
<figcaption>   → caption

This is more meaningful than simply putting everything inside a <div>.